const items0 = [["t72a_par", () => "Your own drug history matters too. If you go manic, psychotic (by which I mean losing your sense of reality to the point of posing a risk to yourself or others), get super paranoid, or end up worse afterward than before, those are signs to reconsider trying the same thing again, and try to figure out why that happened. I'd look at the drug and the dosage, as well as mindset and setting etc. Paranoia during a rough trip can be a warning sign for psychosis, but it doesn't automatically lead there, since it can be valid. It tends to go that way when it isn't balanced by practicality. \"The CIA is mind-controlling me!\" is paranoia. \"The CIA, marketers, and other forces are conspiring to mind-control me, the way they do to everyone else! But since I can see that, I can prevent it to some extent, just by being aware of how it operates\" is paranoia too, but it's practically useful, and less likely to lead to psychosis. \"...which means I can prevent it by standing on my head and counting backwards from 33, since 33 is their magic Freemason number!\" is leaning psychotic. Some drugs are known for causing paranoia, like THC, so I'd avoid those. If you can't clearly understand what happened yourself, get someone qualified to help. And if you have an idea of what happened but aren't sure, ask someone who knows you well.", "cb949804389b3d6c0eaafc8e8db0cab1b0aba26848a381e234f000d60e0b3557"], ["t72a_tr", () => "Sleep paralysis is a trance-like state, and trance can be \"more scary\" because you're trapped in it, but it's also less actually dangerous. You can't ruin your life at all in trance (unless a bad hypnotic suggestion gets deeply implanted, perhaps), while without trance you can \"freak out\" in a way that might end up getting you committed to a mental hospital, thrown out of your housing, going to jail, possibly even killing yourself. In trance you can basically work with the fear. You recognize the fear has no real world significance and it's purely just fear, so it's super useful that way. The thing to fear is fear itself, since it actually opens us up to being manipulated by demons, which feed on it directly. So it's not really that they have no power. We give them the power.", "1eb1992d60de3ba6d25792f83f3865852f225d067d13603448e23451a84b8d9b"], ["t72a_yb5", () => "So how were the next few days? How did you sleep, and could you get through your day okay? Do you want to go back toward what came up, or is some part of you saying no? (And was it hard but doable, or just too much? A lot of the time you just know.) If you honestly can't tell yet, I wouldn't rush to call it healing or harm. I'd just make the next one a bit easier, one change at a time, so you learn what made the difference. Like what? Shorter, maybe, or not as deep, with less imagery. Or at a better time, since right before bed might not be it. \ud83d\ude05 A teddy bear or a doodle can stand in for your little one if picturing them is a lot. Eyes open helps some people, or holding onto something real, like a warm mug. And doing it alone isn't required either. More support around you is a change too.", "3baf330fa747783a1290adf5faef05c6efc329687aa9709547ea165577991e27"], ["t72a_bgout", () => "And can you stop partway through and come back to the room? If you can't, or if going inward is the very thing making it worse, come all the way out first. Look around the room you're actually in, and maybe call somebody. Doing one practical thing to keep yourself safe counts too. A gentler way back in can wait for some other time, like that eyes-open hello. One breath of it may be enough for today. Nobody's handing out medals for how much you can take. \ud83e\udd72", "48311a9aa4b963b696091847ebc7a279cac044a9a1332633b9d1d146536773fb"], ["t72a_bg3", () => "A full break from your little one is a last resort. Sometimes it isn't up to you, though, like when something's actually unsafe. If you do need one, I'd jot down why, and how far you'd gotten before it got to be too much. A couple of lines on your phone is plenty. After a while away, it gets hard to remember. So once that reason has changed and you want to come back, the note tells you where it was still okay. I'd start there, or somewhere a bit gentler.", "b3ccaf7ad9402e6298417e21dc55a702786e081a799690c427d130198dc3cf92"], ["t72a_dgchk", () => "How do you check that? I'd keep whatever you feel around them, and look at their track record. Do they respect a no, or keep pushing on it, and what happens when you disagree? If something outside of them backs up what they're telling you, and it all adds up, that's a good sign.", "9c6ab26829530ff97b43c4f2af84a85c61430f4348b824c27c10dc8ebe5ab8bc"], ["t72a_chkbeh", () => "And the checking doesn't always stay in your head. A body scan can turn into one, or a confession, a date, a hookup, one more late-night search, if what you're really after is proof. That you're lovable, say, or that you're not a bad person. It might calm things down for a while, and then the next little wobble matters even more.", "69d81e8ac5aa19daed83c176327340a33d19a1dbee9058ec2501b098b0086f0e"], ["t72a_alodate", () => "Then there are people you're just getting to know. Meeting them can turn into a kind of test--\"First impressions are everything,\" as they say, but it could be even worse than that, if you need them to prove you're lovable. Nobody's a reassurance machine. Leaning on each other is still a big part of relationships, and it goes both ways. In a real crisis, getting a safe person involved can be exactly the right move. I'm giving you permission now to quit school and flunk the tests. Get curious about who this person is, and who you are, and let both of you have your own reasons for being there in the beautiful present.", "3bdf398208572348aad2cd49419d817f13c7201a204af76a9bc1fcce07f56f49"], ["t72a_ifshs", () => "Is a perfect Higher Self already waiting in there to be your guide or parent? I'd call that claim [spiritual bypassing](https://goenka.u-dont-exist.com/). I've had the Higher Self perspective myself, though. It's a somewhat less subjective way of seeing things as working perfectly in reality, despite the ego's illusions, which matches what people say about it. Where I part ways with IFS is the wisdom part. I don't think the Higher Self has any specific wisdom. It's a vantage point, where more wisdom and compassion can come up, but problems can also look like they were never problems at all from up there, and that can leave you with less of both. You can see both in the guru types who supposedly embody their Higher Self, often in the same person. [Anthony de Mello](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_de_Mello_(Jesuit_priest)) is an excellent example. He was a Jesuit priest who mixed stories from all kinds of traditions into his teaching on seeing through the ego's traps and coming back to the present, and he was a psychotherapist too. He seemed to drift between the two in a way that contradicted itself.", "3aa71f96e79520283e98c51378d441bae5b0fe35eb72db2b704c72f39105b43f"], ["t72a_ifsguide", () => "So inner child therapy draws on those higher vantage points more concretely. The inner Guide is the closest thing here to a Higher Self, and it has to work with the Nurturer and Protector and come after them. It can't act like some transcendent authority that waves everything away. Most reparenting doesn't do much with the Guide at all, which is the opposite problem from IFS, so I'm trying to bring together the best of both. IFS isn't exactly one system, so I may be simplifying it here. It has its own branches and disputes, and that's one more problem I'm trying to remedy by laying all this out systematically in this guide.", "78fbc6779c964182304381ad0253e2481b71e16fb938106a27f2b35ca9c7697e"], ["t72a_st", () => "Of the clinical approaches I know, schema therapy is the closest to this guide. Its therapists do what they call [limited reparenting](https://schematherapysociety.org/Limited-Reparenting): within professional limits, they give you some of what a good parent would, with warmth and firmness and some playfulness too. Over time you take that in, and it becomes your own \"Healthy Adult,\" who looks after your \"Vulnerable Child\" and stands up to the punishing parent in your head. That sounds a lot like the Nurturer and Protector to me, and like borrowed adulthood, since the therapist's version becomes yours bit by bit.", "3e7d758675bb8ed012e8431914df5753a0c7f37a19816a21a33b343fa33da3c1"], ["t72a_stb", () => "The differences are mostly a matter of emphasis, and a lot depends on the therapist. Here it's one grown-up with three jobs, and borrowing just one of them is a fine place to start. Keeping yourself safe right now counts as reparenting from day one, and an image or a body sensation isn't proof of what happened back then. So with a schema therapist, it's worth asking how they'd handle those, and what happens if imagery isn't for you.", "8e5ddc7c32b5d1393cd679b8c4b38fc9d41b66e66555fccb10d1d953a38e99a3"], ["t72a_h1bg", () => "# Before You Try to Go Deep\n\nHow much of the grown-up you is actually here right now? Try looking around the room, pausing before you act, making a choice and following through on it. If you can't do that because you're basically the frightened kid, leave the deeper conversation until later. You may need somebody steady there with you, or to do whatever helps you come back a bit before you try to talk to the child.\n\nAnd if the grown-up you is there, and it's just how deep you're going that's too much, I'd go shallower. Some people try one technique after another instead, to prove to themselves they can take it. Your little one may have had to just take it back then, though. Now there's a grown-up around who can say, \"Okay, that's enough for today,\" and try something gentler instead of giving up on them. Even just saying hi to them with your eyes open, before any imagery, counts.\n\nAnd can you stop partway through and come back to the room? If you can't, or if going inward is the very thing making it worse, come all the way out first. Look around the room you're actually in, and maybe call somebody. Doing one practical thing to keep yourself safe counts too. A gentler way back in can wait for some other time, like that eyes-open hello. One breath of it may be enough for today. Nobody's handing out medals for how much you can take. \ud83e\udd72\n\nA full break from your little one is a last resort. Sometimes it isn't up to you, though, like when something's actually unsafe. If you do need one, I'd jot down why, and how far you'd gotten before it got to be too much. A couple of lines on your phone is plenty. After a while away, it gets hard to remember. So once that reason has changed and you want to come back, the note tells you where it was still okay. I'd start there, or somewhere a bit gentler.\n\n## Your Body Might Need Some Love First\n\nIf you notice tension in your solar plexus or abdomen, or anywhere else that seems to be keeping you from relaxing into that heart chakra love, try some non-forced, slower exhales and a little gentle self-massage. I love using one foot to massage the other foot like a yin-yang symbol, but you can start with some nice circular or spiral massage right on the tense spot.\n\nIf that's not enough, there's deeper somatic stuff like [Shaking](https://www.skool.com/shakingmedicine/about?ref=ee69b2178e96491f88530789dc160291), tapping (EFT), EMDR, Brainspotting, Somatic Experiencing, Water Therapy, Massage, Energy Healing, or some other bottom-up approach.\n\nWhen your nervous system is flooded, your protective parts are busy with the flood, so they're not going to let your little one come out and talk much anyway. Some people go straight into EMDR on their worst memory before they can even stay in the room. Then they get flooded all over again and decide EMDR doesn't work. Here's a map of what to try when:\n\nWhatever you try from that map, I wouldn't go just by how pleasant a session felt to tell whether it was too much. This kind of work can bring up grief and fear, and sometimes hard dreams, or stuff you'd rather never have met. Even staying present the whole time doesn't tell you enough by itself. It's a bit like the gym, where sore and injured can feel pretty similar that evening, and it's the next few days that tell you which one it was.\n\nSo how were the next few days? How did you sleep, and could you get through your day okay? Do you want to go back toward what came up, or is some part of you saying no? (And was it hard but doable, or just too much? A lot of the time you just know.) If you honestly can't tell yet, I wouldn't rush to call it healing or harm. I'd just make the next one a bit easier, one change at a time, so you learn what made the difference. Like what? Shorter, maybe, or not as deep, with less imagery. Or at a better time, since right before bed might not be it. \ud83d\ude05 A teddy bear or a doodle can stand in for your little one if picturing them is a lot. Eyes open helps some people, or holding onto something real, like a warm mug. And doing it alone isn't required either. More support around you is a change too.\n\nBring the challenge back gradually, and only when you want to and you're recovering well. Some people try to toughen themselves up in a cauldron, but what you're looking for is the edge where you can actually learn. And what about those hard dreams, or images that pop up during a session? They can show what feels important or frightening right now. Sometimes they show what feels missing, or what feels needed. They aren't evidence that what you saw in them happened, though, and shouldn't be used to confirm a memory or rebuild one. Or to suggest one.\n\n### Catch the Hook Before the Story Takes Over\n\nPema Ch\u00f6dr\u00f6n calls the tightening and urge you get before the story has finished forming \"shenpa,\" and she says \"[hooked](https://www.lionsroar.com/how-we-get-hooked-shenpa-and-how-we-get-unhooked/)\" is a more descriptive translation than the usual \"attachment.\" You might notice the hook before you have any clue what part of you is doing the hooking. Or you might notice it embarrassingly late. The jaw and stomach are already tight, and/or half the text is written, and then suddenly it dawns on you: you're supposed to be observing yourself. \ud83d\ude00 Maybe forget the parts detective work for a minute. You can figure that out later.\n\nWhen you catch it, look around and just take a moment to enjoy some free honesty: \"Oh! I'm hooked again! That's awesome I could notice it!\" If nobody is in danger, take a breath or three before you finish whatever you were doing. Sometimes human beings need to be being, not always doing. \ud83d\ude43 Maybe then you'll find there's enough adult around to be nice to your little boo for a second. Or enough Protector to stop something you'd regret later.\n\nHere's where I want to clearly depart from the new-age wizdumb that all your problems are self-created: Somebody may really have crossed a boundary. That's not just because they are your karmic partner mirroring what God wants to show you in yourself, even if that also applies sometimes. Your reaction is what you can control the most, though. If what they did hit something old in you, notice how that feels in your body. And notice how your feelings can also amplify or distort your perceptions of others. Just because your stomach tightened up doesn't mean their boundary crossing indicates \u201cThey're evil.\u201d You can defer that judgement for when you're in your happy place.\n\nFeelings about yourself work the same way. \"I feel attracted\" or \"I feel ashamed\" is real as a feeling, but it doesn't settle who you are, or who you love. Making too much out of something momentary is one side of the human psyche's pendulum. Give yourself time to see what patterns form more durably, and what feels authentically yours vs something planted or manipulated into being there from society, influencers, family, friends, religion, etc., etc. You may find an exceptional freedom in exactly that in-between place where you don't have to decide what your pronouns are, or whether you believe in this or that. If you stay in flux forever, though, it can seem like there's nothing solid about you. In that case, sometimes it helps to meditate or talk with a good unbiased therapist who can help you see the pattern.\n\nThe opposite extreme of our pendulum, throwing everything transient away, as some people do with \"thought-noting\" practice, also isn't healthy, except as a meditation exercise. Transient feelings and thoughts can be meaningful signals. For example, if you don't want to be touched, say no now and work out what it means later. Same if something might be dangerous right now, or might be a medical warning sign: step away or get checked first.\n\n### Also Look Outward\n\nOnce you're in your happy place, look at the other person too, and don't go only by the one fight where they crossed your boundary, or, the other way around, by the one \"no\" or boundary of theirs that made you mad \u2014 after all, they're allowed to say no to you. Does it keep ending the same way, where you bring up something that hurt and then still end up reassuring them they're not a bad person, and the talk never comes back to what happened to you? (The psychologist [Lindsay Gibson](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VlNpgFWOLPw) wrote the book on this: [*Disentangling from Emotionally Immature People*](https://www.newharbinger.com/9781648481512/disentangling-from-emotionally-immature-people/).) Or can they hear you disagree and look at their own part?\n\nIf it's the first, it can seem like you haven't explained yourself well enough yet. You probably have, and it makes more sense to count on what they keep showing you they can do than on the right words finally getting through to them. Maybe what they can do is text you something kind the next day and not bring any of it up.\n\nYou may not know for certain whether the other person is or could be capable of a healthier response, and neither do they. If they promise to change, that's probably as useful as their prior promises were. Think about how long it has taken you to become more healthy. Could you have done that from one moment to the next based on a promise? Sometimes we can be a better support for someone when we maintain a realistic boundary, so that we can hold high expectations, and still feel prepared for their failure to meet them.\n\nhttps://substack.com/profile/355021294-u-dont-existcom/note/c-349048098\n\nIf [a relationship](https://romance.u-dont-exist.com) keeps making you feel less safe, meditate on why it makes you feel unsafe, and what keeps you in it. Might it be due to a lack of the healed inner child/inner adult relationship? (If so, you're reading the right guide!) But if they've actually hurt or threatened you, even once, old stuff or not, get safe first, which is not a small thing at all, I know.\n\nIf you really did hurt them, repair it. Also easier said than done, but...\n\nYou can mean the apology for a start. Then notice what happens next. Are you sorry for what you did, or are you starting to take back the complaint too because they're still upset? Maybe your first explanation sucked. Try again. \ud83d\ude05 And if it fails again, and you've said what you mean, well, you can't guarantee they'll understand it. Guilt may keep shouting at you. That doesn't tell you the whole conflict was your fault, nor does it prove they're manipulating you.\n\nThen there are people you're just getting to know. Meeting them can turn into a kind of test--\"First impressions are everything,\" as they say, but it could be even worse than that, if you need them to prove you're lovable. Nobody's a reassurance machine. Leaning on each other is still a big part of relationships, and it goes both ways. In a real crisis, getting a safe person involved can be exactly the right move. I'm giving you permission now to quit school and flunk the tests. Get curious about who this person is, and who you are, and let both of you have your own reasons for being there in the beautiful present.\n\nIf being alone was a big part of what hurt in the first place, then reaching toward people is part of taking care of your little one too. Do you have to get yourself sorted out first, so you'll be better company? I really don't think so. If the hurt was being alone, waiting just means more of being alone. So I'd call somebody or show up somewhere, mess and all.\n\nAnd reaching out doesn't mean telling strangers everything. Privacy isn't fakery. People can earn more of you by how they handle the smaller things you tell them, and how they take it when you say no. But if a relationship is getting deep and you're still keeping up a fake normal, happy version of yourself to hold onto it, I'd either bring more of the truth in, carefully, or let it be less deep for now. (If telling could put you in real danger, like violence, or being outed where that isn't safe, protect yourself first.) And when someone finally accepts you, it can feel amazing. It still doesn't prove you're meant for each other, or that they've earned your trust yet.\n\nI can't tell you where to look for people who fit you, but I'd bet on more than one place. Keep the good people you've already got. Then maybe try a class, or a spiritual community if one really fits you. Not every one of them has to click (nobody's taking attendance). And if any group, or any one person, starts acting like they should be your only support, that's where I'd want the Protector paying attention.\n\n### Write It. Don't Send It Yet.\n\nIf you've got something burning you up that you wanna say, write it out as a reply. There. That got it out. Ahh. Breathing easier. \ud83d\ude0c But don't send it. Take that breather you earned and then come back to it from a cooler place.\n\nIt doesn't mean you need to hide your upset, or apply for a permit to protest, but choose your battles and execute them wisely, so you don't end up regretting how you communicated later. As Lao Tsu might have said (if I could ever have read that whole boring Art of War book), a war with no casualties is the best possible war. When high IQ & EQ communication hits a writer's block, sometimes the best thing is to simply say, \"I need some time to think about my response.\" And if the conflict really requires a response right now, then obviously just do your best rather than wait for the perfect timing.\n\nAnd yeah, going back to the writer, which is often my inner child when emotions pop up, is part of the process. It's like I'm holding his hand and showing him a better way, not like I just stole his pencil because he wrote something embarrassing. So what was he actually trying to say? A lot of the time it's just \"that hurt.\"\n\nOther times it's \"I did this too.\" My friend Tyler saw a guy smoking in front of a baby and asked him if he thought that was wise. The guy asked if he thought it was wise to mind his own business, and Tyler said, \"Well, I believe in Christ, so I thought you might care about life too.\" Afterward he wasn't sure he'd handled it right, or whether he should have thrown the first stone at all, since he'd smoked in front of a baby himself.\n\nBut Jesus also said to take the log out of your own eye first, and then \"[you will see clearly to take the speck out of your brother's eye](https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Matthew%207%3A3-5&version=ESV).\" That's exactly what made Tyler the right person to say something. If you actually have the problem they have, and you want to call them out for it, call yourself out for it instead. What we came up with was something like, \"Hey man, when I was younger I smoked in front of an infant, not realizing how selfish that was, and I notice you doing the same thing. I'd ask you to please have some compassion for him. I have a young child now, so this really touches me.\"\n\nIt's also how your voice sounds, though. You can say all the right words, but if the tone is accusatory, it doesn't matter. Tyler's an evangelical Christian who preaches on YouTube, so it meant a lot to me that he liked this take on what Jesus was saying.\n\n### When Healing Turns Into Checking\n\nYou can get hooked on healing too. The wound can be completely real while you're checking it for the hundredth time to see whether you've finally grieved it correctly.\n\nGoing back can still matter, since realizations are a real part of life. But returning to the same wound can also become a search for certainty in an inherently uncertain existence. Spending all day asking, \"Did I process it properly? Did I grieve it enough?\" is clearly not what we're after here.\n\nAnd the checking doesn't always stay in your head. A body scan can turn into one, or a confession, a date, a hookup, one more late-night search, if what you're really after is proof. That you're lovable, say, or that you're not a bad person. It might calm things down for a while, and then the next little wobble matters even more.\n\nIf you're feeling guilty because you didn't put your seat belt on, just put it on. If you still want to investigate it later, fine. Perhaps now and then you feel bad about that time you refused to put it on, broke your collar bone in a crash, and then got blamed for not wearing it. That's natural, but doesn't mean there's something new to look at. Then the background checker goes, \u201cBut are you really listening?\u201d There it is. Seat belt's on. \ud83d\ude07 Where were we?\n\nDon't be afraid to ask your therapist/yourself \"What is this practice supposed to help, and how would I know if it's helping?\" This sovereign, gently curious stance should apply to everything in life. Feeling calmer or having deeper insights or explanations for the trauma response are some possible signs of initial progress, but if that's the endgame, it's just a cope. Real healing is ultimately measured by reduced need for the therapy. It's like real parenting: you might start out reading parenting books and thinking hard about what to say or do, but after a while you get into the groove and a lot of it becomes natural. Inner parenting can be like that too. You don't need to keep checking this guide or planning every conversation with your little one; the nurturing, protecting and guiding become more automatic. You might still turn to therapy when you need it, and life will keep offering chances to grow wiser and more compassionate. And if the therapy is actually working, it should improve your daily life, and especially the health of the inner child-inner adult relationship. Going at it harder isn't as useful as going at it with more wisdom and compassion. Take the parts that work, and innovate on the rest.\n\nIf these exercises start feeling like a ritual you have to complete before you're allowed to live, stop there.\n\n## You Don\u2019t Need an Inner Monologue\n\nIf you don't hear a little kid talking in your head, don't invent one. Actually, this is one place where the usual inner-child prompt to \u201cask your little one what they need\u201d can get confusing, because \u201cask\u201d sounds like you're supposed to ask a question and then wait for a second voice to answer. Maybe that's what happens for some people, but you might not be some people (hehe).\n\nYou might barely have spontaneous words in your mind and yet still be able to think a sentence on purpose. Try it and see. If words are there, speak or write them. You might feel more comfortable that way than just staying in imagination land.  And if that starts feeling like homework you're doing for a child who isn't answering, drop it. Maybe what's actually requiring your attention now is a knot in your stomach. Or perhaps you're in a place you want to leave. Sometimes nothing \u201canswers\u201d and you just know what would be nice to do for yourself.\n\nAnd it might not be the same order all the time. You could have a feeling before thoughts, or thoughts might come first and provoke a feeling. They say hindsight is 20/20, but you actually might not even know which happened first. That's ok, too.\n\n## When the Present-Day Adult Is Dangerous to the Child\n\nI've been writing this whole guide as if everybody who tries it wants to take care of the inner child. Some people get a little rush out of making somebody scared or small, though. For such folks, inner child therapy is clearly not the place to start. They need to first deconstruct the adult identity they've got.\n\nWhen I talk with folks like that, I mostly want to know what the identity gets them and what it costs, and especially what it's really serving. Some of them even call themselves sovereign, and then somehow there's an urge they have to obey, which sounds more like slavery to me.\n\nThis part tends to scare the wrong people, like somebody who's horrified by their own thoughts. Everybody gets scary thoughts they'd rather not have sometimes, and they don't make you a bad person, but why would you bring your inner child into the middle of them? The nice thing about inner child therapy is that you can wait until you're calm and actually ready. A real parent doesn't get to do that, which is one reason nobody manages to be a perfect one.\n\nAnd if somebody actually wonders whether they're malicious, they'd do better to meditate on it and find out why they're asking before they bring their inner child anywhere near it. Are they really feeling malicious, or is it some voice in their head saying so, like the inherited critic? If some of it turns out to be malice they actually want to act on, the inner child can wait.\n\nIn one of the Buddha's own accounts of how he woke up, ill will shows up in his mind and he sees it's hurting him. If he had to figure that out by watching, it's not obvious. Ill will can make you feel powerful. Being on fire would too, and people would definitely get out of your way, but unless you're the Human Torch from the Fantastic Four, you'd be the one burning. So it's worth noticing what it's doing to you. Has it ever left anybody at ease, with nothing to regret afterward? Of course not.\n\nThe Buddha also told his son that loving-kindness is what gets rid of ill will, and compassion does the same for cruelty. I'm biased on loving-kindness, since it got me out of my depression. For these folks it starts with the grown-up they are now, though, and their little one stays out of it.\n\nAs for when their little one gets a turn, I'd say once they've had some bad days where scaring somebody would've felt good, and they didn't do it. Until then it helps a lot to have a real person in their life who likes them and won't laugh along, since loving-kindness is easier to pick up from someone who already has some.", "355a26224f52427e259179a0f3cf586b417d371cff23544aff5b98dbe4c249c5"], ["t72a_h1wavf", () => "# When the Adult Voice Feels Fake\n\nA lot of people get stuck right here. You call up the Nurturer and try to talk to your little one, and you get silence or a numb feeling, and all of a sudden your phone or the laundry feels urgent. Or some voice says this is silly, and it's hard to tell which part of you is saying it.\n\nMy friend Scott said:\n\n> \"Half the time when I try to connect to baby me, I feel fake and it seems crazy.\"\n\nI used to be all personal trainer and teach persistence on this as the remedy. Keeping at it does help, since a little one who learned to expect being dropped won't trust you right away. But I don't think that explains the adult feeling fully. More importantly, I think it could even be like unintentional gaslighting to the little one to ask to connect with them when you don't believe they're even real. Like when my mom pretended to listen to me when I was young, but even less excusable (from the inner child's view) because she was not asking for my time when she did that. So, it's worth meditating first on why it feels fake.\n\nSometimes kindness feels scary more than fake. One study's title has a word for that: \"[compassion-threat](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31301164/).\" People in it with anxious or avoidant attachment reacted to compassion imagery like a threat, and a ten-minute exercise in feeling secure helped with the next try. [Another](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25347984/) found a first try at imagery like that \"can be frightening for people who have a tendency to be self-critical.\" So feeling fake could mean you need a bridge, more than a bigger performance of love.\n\nAnd picturing one ideal parent seems to help some people. There's a whole method built on it, called the Ideal Parent Figure method. In a [small pilot](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5700488/), seventeen adults with complex PTSD from childhood trauma did five weeks of it and were still doing better eight months later. Seventeen people with nobody to compare them to won't prove the whole method, but it's a start.\n\nFor some people it only stops feeling fake in an altered state, like on MDMA or deep in meditation. All of a sudden your little one is right there! Oh my gosh! And the love or grief that sounded like therapy talk is just obvious. Old memories might come up too. They can feel totally real, but that alone doesn't prove they happened. I'd still hang on to what you felt, even once it's gone, and try to bring a little of it into your normal life. You've felt that your little one is real now, so talking to them afterward isn't just pretending.\n\n## The Parent You Inherited\n\nI'd look at the parent \"script\" you inherited: how you learned to treat yourself from how people treated you growing up, long before you started building your Nurturer, Protector and Guide. It can be warm, but it's often more like a critic, or a warden with rules. The rules can even sound reasonable, like \"You can come back to the table when you're done with your embarrassing crying fit.\" If yours was like that, your little one learned bit by bit to hide that part of their authentic self which wasn't allowed, so they could keep belonging. They might still be doing it.\n\nIf it's a critic, the parent you inherited also measures whatever you show the world against an impossible ideal, and then attacks you from both sides: you're not good enough, and anything that does look good is fake anyway. That second one means nothing you do could ever count, and it's one place feeling fake can come from. When a new grown-up voice shows up saying \"Trust me,\" of course your little one doesn't answer.\n\nBut with the borrowed adulthood trick, the new grown-up can start outside your head, as someone who likes you the way you are, or as something you do to protect your little one, like letting the phone ring when you know how that call goes. You don't have to pretend to feel anything to let a phone ring. Your little one can watch you do it many times before trusting you, while the parent you inherited calls it fake.\n\n## Start With Whatever Showed Up\n\nYour little one wants to play, too, which is why I call the deeper therapy pl/ork, play + work. A lot of the best discoveries come when you're both just messing around, kind of like how kids tell you what's really going on from the back seat, when nobody's looking right at them. Play not only paves the way for therapy, but is a part of how successful therapy should be measured.\n\nA lot of what adults tend to lose, your little one still has, like the silliness and the irreverence, the rawness, the creativity, all that, and maybe a connection to the Great Mystery from before any path gave it a name. That's the spark of life they bring, and you get to draw on it and give back, so it's more communion than command. For one example of the spark, there's my post [Treasuring the Inner Child's Gift](https://ibogaqueen.substack.com/p/treasuring-the-inner-childs-gift).\n\nYou might go looking for your little one and get mad instead, or realize you've been staring at the rug. I count that as pl/ork too, even if it feels like it's in the way. Before you decide where it's coming from, ask it what it's trying to stop, or what it wants. Maybe it just has something to say. It could be your little one, mad that you took so long to come back, or a part of you that's trying to protect you. Maybe it's that parent you inherited, or something from earlier today, or a bit of each.\n\nHow you picture all that is up to you, as long as it helps, and the simpler the better. I think a fear that keeps turning up to tell you something is worth talking to as a part of you. If it sounds like your little one, though, hear it out the first time it shows up. Let a stray thought go as just thinking, and come back to the room you're in. A worry that just keeps asking to be reassured can go the same way, unless it's your little one asking.\n\nI wouldn't spend long deciding what to call each part, though. Every part of you learns from how the others treat it, your little one included. A part of you that's trying to protect you might warn you about something, and when you hear it out, it doesn't have to get any louder.\n\nYou can tell that part something like, \"I see you. I'm not going to force my way past you. What's going on?\" Or however you'd actually say that. Then wait and listen patiently. If nothing comes, that's fine for now. No need to answer for that part just because you'd set this up as a healing session. Even without the answer, you can notice what sets the reaction off, and you can understand what the reaction is propelling you toward, and away from.\n\nOr if you do get an answer, it could come in different forms, like as a voice or just a hunch. It's often more like a feeling. Maybe grief or anger. If so just listen and be present for it if that's what it wants. If it tells you to do something, I'd only do it if it's loving and leaves you free. It should make sense, too. Even then, anything big or hard to undo can wait until you're calm and you've checked it against what you actually know (if you're in danger, get safe first).\n\nDaniel Kahneman and Gary Klein came at intuition from two camps of research that were supposed to be at odds, and the paper they wrote together is called [Conditions for Intuitive Expertise: A Failure to Disagree](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0016755). They start from intuition being \"sometimes marvelous and sometimes flawed.\" Yours too, probably. So which kind is a given hunch? How sure it feels won't tell you. Their answer, roughly, is to ask whether the situation is predictable at all, and whether you've had a real chance to learn it. A hunch can pick up on a pattern before your head can explain it, so hear it out, and then let your head have a look. Neither one should decide alone, though. Your head can get tired of not knowing and be glad to hand the whole thing over, to a hunch or to somebody who sounds sure.\n\nAnxiety deserves the same kind of listening. A lot of the time it's a part of you that's trying to protect you, making a guess about what's coming: that you'll get overwhelmed, maybe, or that people will laugh at you. It can be right! It's probably been wrong before too, and you know some of those times. So when it's safe to find out, I'd hear it out and then go see. Maybe nobody laughs. (Or they do, and you survive it.) If it might be real danger, though, I'd get some distance first, and find out how right it was later. Same with something a doctor should look at.\n\nSome of it I wouldn't test at all, even when it sounds like a prediction. If a part hesitates about being touched or about sex, that's enough to stop. And if it says no to going deeper in this work, or to an altered state, I'd stop there too. It might not be able to tell you why.\n\n### Two Common Protective Patterns\n\nTwo kinds of reaction really are trying to protect you, more often than not. The first one is on duty all the time, and it can sound like a skeptic: \"You're just making this up.\" It may have spent years keeping you from being humiliated again, or left again. That's a long time to stay on guard. It might be tired by now, but it's experienced and good at what it does. It has probably watched you get excited about other healing things, then drop them.\n\nThe other one shows up when you get close to something painful, as a sudden pull to do anything but feel it (even a yawn can come with an \"I'm so done with this\" feeling). It might believe there's no grown-up around who could hold what's coming up. I'd want out too. That yawn might actually be the opposite of checking out, though. Re-evaluation Counseling, a kind of peer counseling, counts yawning as emotional discharge, a feeling on its way out. The skeptic and the pull both started out trying to prevent something that really hurt, and they can keep at it even when what they do now hurts you. If you treat either one like an enemy, or like proof that something's wrong with you, it'll probably just think \"See? I knew it,\" and stay in charge. I wonder what it would do if it didn't have to.\n\n### Hear the Protective Part First\n\nWhichever protective part showed up, try talking to it in the same Nurturer voice you meant for your little one. A harder voice can seem more fitting for something that's been in your way. I'd still keep it the same. If that voice isn't there in you yet, borrow it from someone who's been safe for you. At some point you can ask that part, \"What are you afraid would happen if you stepped back?\" It might say this will just be one more performance, and you'll fool yourself again.\n\nThat one can sting, because there could be something to it. Maybe you've fooled yourself before, and that part remembers. \"Tell me what you remember, I'm listening.\" works well here. It puts you into the role of the Nurturer, which alleviates the instinct to argue back. Usually your little one only comes forward after that part feels heard. Peek-a-boo! If you push past it, you might get exactly the performance it was afraid of. Even if you didn't win an Emmy last year, that can look convincing, with the right words and maybe even tears, while nothing underneath feels any safer.\n\nA new roommate doesn't win you over with a speech on move-in day (hopefully, right?). The rent just has to keep showing up. Your Protector, the grown-up you, earns that protective part's trust about the same way, mostly out in ordinary life. The things you do can be small, like keeping a promise to yourself nobody else knew about. Or saying no to someone, and not texting an hour later to take it back. That second one can be really hard.\n\nThat part doesn't have to move out. It's been living there way longer than the grown-up you. It can keep watch while you take one small step toward your little one, and I think watching is what it wants anyway, to find out if there's a grown-up here now who could call it off if things got to be too much. If it says no, I'd listen. When it lets you, keep an eye on that part too, for any change at all. If it's been calling all this stupid, it might turn out to be scared of getting its hopes up. I wouldn't want to get mine up either. I'd tell it something like, \"You don't have to hope yet, I'll keep coming back even when I mess up.\" Sometimes it doesn't say anything back for a while.\n\n### When to Change the Strategy\n\nWeeks in and nothing much is changing? I'd want to quit too, or else keep doing the exact same thing forever so I never had to find out. Before either one, I'd look for what exactly is in the way. Sometimes those weeks were mostly reading about it and getting ready, which can feel a lot like doing it (this article included). Or you did sit down with your little one, and fear got there first, or a part of you that's trying to protect you. It can be as plain as never having the place to yourself. Whatever it turns out to be, I'd work on that one thing instead of giving up on everything.\n\nMaybe you've been trying to comfort your little one for weeks, and the words are all there, but the loving grown-up behind them isn't, not yet. Some people at that point might try saying sweeter and sweeter things, and then upon that not working, perhaps just \"throw the baby out with the bathwater,\" and decide reparenting is useless. I'd borrow that grown-up first. Then try again, and see if your little one can tell the difference.\n\nOne more thing about telling whether it helped. A few hours of feeling lighter, with the old feeling back the same day, is a few hours of relief. I'd take that! But I wouldn't decide from it that the problem's over, or even that the exercise did it, especially if you also slept well for once. Write down what changed and how long it lasted if you want, and then leave it alone.\n\nHow many tries is enough? I really don't know, and I'd be suspicious of anyone who promises you a number, including you, since some things shift in a day and some take about as long as learning guitar. If it's making things worse, or you just can't do it right now, I'd change course sooner (stopping counts). Not wanting to do it anymore is a reason too, but if that \"nope\" sounds like a part of you that's trying to protect you, I'd hear it out first. If it still says no, that's your answer. And if you keep following the reparenting steps correctly without getting the expected results, consider if you have a unique case these steps don't match yet. Maybe you'll find something that works better for you, or you can ask me what I'd do for this case, or ask a therapist you work with. Either way, I'd like to hear about it!\n\nSometimes the session itself goes really well. You feel real care for your little one and say exactly what they needed to hear. And a few days later you're skipping lunch and saying yes to everything again. A lot of people's first guess is that they didn't go deep enough. I don't think that's it, though, and I don't think this one failed either. Maybe the grown-up just hasn't come out of the exercise with you yet. Whatever you did for your little one in there, I'd try a tiny bit of it out here, like texting a friend that you're having a rough day instead of saying you're fine. That can honestly be harder than the meditation was. One small thing like that can teach your little one more than ten more good meditations. It doesn't have to happen after every session, and once isn't proof you've got it all down.\n\n### When the Urge to Escape Arrives\n\nSometimes the urge to get away from what's coming up gets a lot bigger. All of a sudden you need a drink, or that ex doesn't seem so bad after all. Some people start a whole new business. If that's you, it might mean you got close to something that really hurts, and that can be progress, honestly! I'd thank whatever part of you came up with the escape plan, and slow down. If it's more than you can hold right now, I'd get someone steady with you.\n\nSo why not just blast right past that part, with intensive meditation or psychedelics? Both can get you past the guards before there's a grown-up big enough to hold whatever comes through, so I'd hold off until there's enough Nurturer and Protector around. How much is enough depends on the drug. Like MDMA, San Pedro is more protective and nurturing than many other drugs, especially at lower doses, so you don't need as much Nurturer and Protector built up for it, though you still need some. I already had some from my good childhood to draw on. The first time I took San Pedro cactus, at a medium-low dose, it made me super emotional, and I cried a lot. It was very therapeutic, actually. But I still squandered that opportunity: with a better understanding of reparenting, it could have been much more therapeutic.\n\nAyahuasca was often much more challenging, because at first I was taking huge doses for ego death. The ayahuasca by itself wasn't exactly spiritual bypassing for me, though. It gave me a connection to divinity, and inner-child reparenting does work best with a little of that. But then I squandered the opportunity to bring that love down from the heavens to my inner child, which led me to be more like a preacher than a fully well-rounded individual who could lead by example.\n\nAll kinds of psychoactive drugs can mess with your sense of what's real, and that's often the point of them. But in general, try to select the healthiest ones and use slow and gentle protocols. I'd say one of the most abused psychedelics (aside from ketamine, if you call that a psychedelic) is 5-MeO-DMT. I have a [guide on that](http://bufo.u-dont-exist.com/). People very often believe you need a huge dose of it for a \"full release.\" They go through all kinds of screaming and shaking, nearly killing themselves, with absolutely zero inner-child work being done. It's all spiritual bypassing.\n\nYour own drug history matters too. If you go manic, psychotic (by which I mean losing your sense of reality to the point of posing a risk to yourself or others), get super paranoid, or end up worse afterward than before, those are signs to reconsider trying the same thing again, and try to figure out why that happened. I'd look at the drug and the dosage, as well as mindset and setting etc. Paranoia during a rough trip can be a warning sign for psychosis, but it doesn't automatically lead there, since it can be valid. It tends to go that way when it isn't balanced by practicality. \"The CIA is mind-controlling me!\" is paranoia. \"The CIA, marketers, and other forces are conspiring to mind-control me, the way they do to everyone else! But since I can see that, I can prevent it to some extent, just by being aware of how it operates\" is paranoia too, but it's practically useful, and less likely to lead to psychosis. \"...which means I can prevent it by standing on my head and counting backwards from 33, since 33 is their magic Freemason number!\" is leaning psychotic. Some drugs are known for causing paranoia, like THC, so I'd avoid those. If you can't clearly understand what happened yourself, get someone qualified to help. And if you have an idea of what happened but aren't sure, ask someone who knows you well.\n\nI went psychotic once on ayahuasca made with pure THH (an alkaloid from the ayahuasca vine) and mimosa hostilis bark, which has DMT in it. THH alone isn't hypnotic (trance-like) enough to keep people safe with higher doses of DMT. When you're in a trance, you can't freak out: no matter how scary something is, it's just like, \"Oh, this is scary, OK, oh well.\" That's not a good excuse to take crazy doses tho. I didn't need anyone to explain that to me, because it was obvious afterward, although it was very helpful that my landlord, a Quaker family friend, was super understanding and talked me down gently. Otherwise the cops might have been called, and I could still be in jail, perhaps, since ayahuasca was illegal there at the time, not to mention I had a bunch of mimosa bark in the house that they might have claimed was for distributing. In general, don't call the cops and expect it to go well. That's a real last resort.\n\nOut of hundreds of times I've taken iboga, there was one time just a small amount of male iboga root bark got me a bit hypomanic, to the point where a thought came: \"Everyone should listen to me!\" Luckily I was trained in Buddhist meditation, so I immediately challenged it with \"Why should anyone listen if they have no interest?\" I stopped using iboga for a while after that, and I warn people about that potential issue with [male iboga](http://iboga.u-dont-exist.com/) specifically.\n\nAny of those reactions, from mania to paranoia, can also point to a vulnerability in you, so I'd be careful with other drugs that mess with what's real too, though I don't mean every drug's dangerous for you. And any predisposition toward mania or psychosis is a warning sign too, whether it's family history, a bad environment or a bad mindset. I can't tell you exactly what to do about it, and nobody can tell you for sure what's best for you. There are just caution signs, and when you see them, you take more precautions of every kind. You have to figure out what they mean for you, and if you can't, get help from trusted advisors.\n\nBy trusted, I mean someone you know is trustworthy from a long relationship with them, not someone people on Reddit said was super magical and healing. That's called a crapshoot. It might turn out well, it might not. It might seem like it did, and then later you realize you were duped, as happened to me with [Sidney the iboga shaman](http://sidney.u-dont-exist.com/). A new therapist is not as trusted as an old one. As they say, \"new friends are silver, but old friends are gold.\"\n\nMilder reactions afterward count as caution signs too, like getting confused or spaced out (dissociating) again and again. Get professional help right away if any of this is severe or risky, and especially if it keeps going or comes back while you're sober. If you can't tell whether you're safe, I'd treat that as risky. Don't suddenly quit a prescribed medicine, or anything your body is dependent on, just because it might be part of the problem; have a doctor help you stop safely.\n\nBe careful what you mix, too, and not just prescribed medicines. Even some basic things like cinnamon can be dangerous (and helpful) with ayahuasca, and doctors don't know about a lot of this. I also learned that mixing acacia confusa with mimosa hostilis is very dangerous. I'd never recommend anyone risk it, since it's highly destabilizing and challenging. I have a [list of things to be cautious about with ayahuasca](http://ayasafety.u-dont-exist.com/) in particular.\n\nGentle meditation or self-hypnosis is a different story. It can really calm your nervous system down. A lot of people find that a mantra they feel some heart connection to, repeated in their head or out loud, especially with a nice melody, can lower the walls enough for the heart to open up. It's not something to lean on all day as a way of coping, but used as therapy it can be super effective. I personally love this beautiful Om Mane Pemme Hum mantra that I found in the Garchen Rinpoche documentary, For the Benefit of All Beings:", "e959f0235d16d1c00609608f428f6b70bea7efc4ff0de557ebbe8456e3d34582"], ["t72a_h1as", () => "# Altered States Can Deepen the Therapy\n\nI'd learn to do inner-child therapy sober first, in your ordinary mindstate, since that's the one you'll be living in. Gentle meditation or self-hypnosis can help with that from the start, like I said about the urge to escape. When you\u2019re ready, my [self-hypnosis guide](http://hypnosis.u-dont-exist.com/) or [lucid-dreaming guide](http://lucid.u-dont-exist.com/) can take it deeper. Low doses of entheogens can also synergize with it: cactus, CBD tea with sublingual mapacho, Loveyhuasca, [iboga](http://iboga.u-dont-exist.com/), and so on.\n\nRemember those guards from the urge to escape? Same deal here, so get enough Nurturer and Protector in the room before you go deep. Borrowed is fine, as long as it's from someone steady who won't try to run your trip for you. And yes, those caution signs still count. But feeling attacked by a demon on a psychedelic is not necessarily paranoia. It can be real, and it's also not bad unless you're unprepared. It can be supremely empowering when you can send love and see how it fixes the situation.\n\nNow, the beautiful stuff. Say one night (on a psychedelic, in a dream, ten days into a meditation retreat, whatever) you felt more love than you knew was possible. Wonderful, and it matters! Keep it as a reference point, no need to keep chasing it. Feeling it once is a long way from feeling it only when you're tripping, and that's still a long way from feeling it on a regular morning while the coffee brews.\n\nJack Kornfield has a whole book on what comes next, [After the Ecstasy, the Laundry](https://jackkornfield.com/after-the-ecstasy-the-laundry/). The title kind of says it all. That night can't be the bar for every morning after, or regular life starts to feel like failing. And gorgeous as it was, it doesn't mean you're done healing. The laundry will tell you more, and it's the same test as back in Timing Is Individual.\n\nAnd if your little one still can't take the love in? Hold off on going bigger, however tempting it is. Something's in the way, and getting to know it is your next job. Often it's a protective part that's seen love come with strings before. Hear it out first, the way you did in Hear the Protective Part First.\n\n## Altered States Can Require Reparenting\n\nHave you ever been attacked by a literal demon? Maybe during sleep paralysis or meditation or on a psychedelic trip? A lot of modern folk laugh at this, yet it\u2019s really quite a common experience. I was being psychically attacked by ghosts in my home as a child. Because I was literally a child, I was an easy target. And since most people never actually grew up, they are easy targets for every kind of influence, including spirit influence.\n\nThose ghosts knew my weakness: dares. So they would dare me to jump out the window. I wasn\u2019t suicidal, but I hated to seem like a \u201cwussy.\u201d Luckily, I trusted my parents with this information, and instead of taking me to a psychiatrist and pumping me full of anti-psychotic drugs, they put bars on the windows. Many kids are not so lucky in modern societies. They may get pumped full of anti-psychotic drugs for the rest of their life once diagnosed. That said, it'd make sense to get a professional to weigh in for some situations. I still played around with half-hanging out the windows, but I wasn\u2019t quite dumb enough to jump out, and I really appreciate that my parents took it seriously without catastrophizing.\n\nAs a semi-adult, age 26, I discovered meditation. After I began meditating deeply for a while, it seemed like my astral portal opened. I started getting attacked by spirits during sleep paralysis. They would sit on me, prevent me from breathing, and instill tremendous fear into the core of my being.\n\nYep. Exactly like that.\n\nSleep paralysis is a trance-like state, and trance can be \"more scary\" because you're trapped in it, but it's also less actually dangerous. You can't ruin your life at all in trance (unless a bad hypnotic suggestion gets deeply implanted, perhaps), while without trance you can \"freak out\" in a way that might end up getting you committed to a mental hospital, thrown out of your housing, going to jail, possibly even killing yourself. In trance you can basically work with the fear. You recognize the fear has no real world significance and it's purely just fear, so it's super useful that way. The thing to fear is fear itself, since it actually opens us up to being manipulated by demons, which feed on it directly. So it's not really that they have no power. We give them the power.\n\nAfter multiple episodes of this I decided to stop giving in to the victim mentality, and to start taking it seriously as a part of spiritual practice. My inner guide saw it as a perfect opportunity to demonstrate the power of love over fear. The next time it happened, despite how scary it was, I sent unconditional love to this demon on me. It took some minutes, but once that love was seen as enduring, the demon left, and it never returned.\n\nI\u2019ve also trained other people to do this during spiritual attack while on psychedelics. It works a lot better if you are already trained in [loving-kindness meditation](https://love.u-dont-exist.com/) to the point where it\u2019s natural. I\u2019d say it works way better than exorcism techniques (like [Julian Palmer\u2019s torping](https://julianpalmerism.com/primer-malefic-entities/), which he also admits doesn\u2019t work as well as love).\n\nThe astral plane is a bit different from the 3D. In the astral realm, unconditional love is a true, ultimately powerful physical force. Like a river, or a moat. There\u2019s no evil that can trespass that moat. But don\u2019t assume it will always work like that in the physical plane. It definitely has worked for me sometimes when facing some thugs, but [definitely not with others](https://defense.u-dont-exist.com/). Loving someone does not mean you leave the front door unlocked with a sign saying \u201ctake whatever you want.\u201d\n\nIf you\u2019re not able yet (or in the moment) to summon real love for an enemy, you can first send love to your frightened little one from your adult Nurturer. Many people also find they can borrow support from God, Jesus, angels, devas, or whatever loving spiritual presence fits their tradition. If I were to do that, I would still be framing it as \u201cPlease help this situation with love\u201d rather than \u201cStrike down the enemy!\u201d But if a presence seems to enjoy your fear, it's not one of your grown-ups, borrowed or not, whatever it says it is. Same goes if it demands you hurt or use anybody, yourself included.\n\nBorrowing support like that is basically borrowed adulthood. At first you may need to feel protected or loved by something bigger than you, before that love starts to feel like yours, and then bit by bit it starts to become your own. Send some of it down to your frightened little one, and when that's possible, toward whatever you're afraid of. Continuing to relate to benevolent spiritual powers/beings is still perfectly fine, but they would surely be happy if they rubbed off on you!\n\nIf a voice or urge is directing you towards harm, and you feel at risk of acting on it, as with myself and those ghosts at home, move away from that open window and get some outside support. Know the limit of what you can handle, but don't turn it into a prison for life.\n\n#### Non-altered states can feel like this too\n\nEven social media algorithms will turn your life into hell if you don\u2019t have the loving, protective inner parent developed. When you do, social media actually becomes useful and feels nice to interact with now and then.\n\nMany people think reparenting sounds silly, or like the kind of ridiculous therapy people do when they have too much time or money. But you know what\u2019s even sillier? Wandering the world aimlessly and getting manipulated by all kinds of evil forces. \ud83d\udc80", "8e58a774c6ad38fb62fbe828882f0ba94dc28d3ffb641cff2f5c8556560ab120"], ["t72a_h1app", () => "# Appendix\n\n## How This Relates to Schema Therapy\n\nOf the clinical approaches I know, schema therapy is the closest to this guide. Its therapists do what they call [limited reparenting](https://schematherapysociety.org/Limited-Reparenting): within professional limits, they give you some of what a good parent would, with warmth and firmness and some playfulness too. Over time you take that in, and it becomes your own \"Healthy Adult,\" who looks after your \"Vulnerable Child\" and stands up to the punishing parent in your head. That sounds a lot like the Nurturer and Protector to me, and like borrowed adulthood, since the therapist's version becomes yours bit by bit.\n\nThe differences are mostly a matter of emphasis, and a lot depends on the therapist. Here it's one grown-up with three jobs, and borrowing just one of them is a fine place to start. Keeping yourself safe right now counts as reparenting from day one, and an image or a body sensation isn't proof of what happened back then. So with a schema therapist, it's worth asking how they'd handle those, and what happens if imagery isn't for you.\n\n## How This Relates to IFS\n\nRichard Schwartz, who developed Internal Family Systems (IFS), called one of his books [No Bad Parts](https://ifs-institute.com/store/419). Honor, understand and love every part, he says, and the protective ones are no exception, so \"meet the protector first\" would fit right in over there. That's a lot of the overlap with this method, right there in the title.\n\nMy reparenting method diverges from IFS at an important point. The IFS Institute's [outline of the model](https://ifs-institute.com/resources/articles/internal-family-systems-model-outline) says \"everyone has a Self,\" and that the Self \"can and should lead,\" once your parts stop \"blending\" with it. I emphasize the inner adult you build and practice occupying: Nurturer, Protector, and Leader, Guide, or Guru. That's still one grown-up with three jobs, like back in The Chicken-and-Egg Problem.\n\nCan't find a calm, compassionate Self in there? Then borrow. One grown-up job at a time, from a friend who'll play Protector for a night, or from something you do, like letting the phone ring when you know how that call goes (The Parent You Inherited again), until it's yours.\n\nIs a perfect Higher Self already waiting in there to be your guide or parent? I'd call that claim [spiritual bypassing](https://goenka.u-dont-exist.com/). I've had the Higher Self perspective myself, though. It's a somewhat less subjective way of seeing things as working perfectly in reality, despite the ego's illusions, which matches what people say about it. Where I part ways with IFS is the wisdom part. I don't think the Higher Self has any specific wisdom. It's a vantage point, where more wisdom and compassion can come up, but problems can also look like they were never problems at all from up there, and that can leave you with less of both. You can see both in the guru types who supposedly embody their Higher Self, often in the same person. [Anthony de Mello](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_de_Mello_(Jesuit_priest)) is an excellent example. He was a Jesuit priest who mixed stories from all kinds of traditions into his teaching on seeing through the ego's traps and coming back to the present, and he was a psychotherapist too. He seemed to drift between the two in a way that contradicted itself.\n\nSo inner child therapy draws on those higher vantage points more concretely. The inner Guide is the closest thing here to a Higher Self, and it has to work with the Nurturer and Protector and come after them. It can't act like some transcendent authority that waves everything away. Most reparenting doesn't do much with the Guide at all, which is the opposite problem from IFS, so I'm trying to bring together the best of both. IFS isn't exactly one system, so I may be simplifying it here. It has its own branches and disputes, and that's one more problem I'm trying to remedy by laying all this out systematically in this guide.\n\nIFS calls these wounded young parts \u201cexiles.\u201d I find that pathologizing. I prefer to speak of a child who needs parenting and integration, rather than naming them by their wounded or excluded condition. Then there's \"unburdening,\" often done after you've witnessed a part's origin story. It can be powerful, sure. It can also get dramatic, all memories and plenty of room for suggestion, which can knock you off balance, especially if you tend to dissociate (DID-like fragmentation included) or to spiritually bypass.\n\nAnd the parts just keep coming: managers, firefighters, exiles, polarized parts, legacy burdens, Self-energy, guides, new subparts... At some point it's a maze, with a little one somewhere in the middle of it. A gentle practitioner can walk you through it fine. With others, people seem to end up more fragmented than when they started, and an institute with certifications behind the model doesn't by itself make it any truer, or safer for that matter.\n\nBefore choosing a therapist, get to know the strengths and pitfalls of IFS and reparenting. Could an IFS therapist help? They may, if they're skilled and grounded. You'd want one who's trauma-informed and doesn't put ideas in your head, especially one who understands dissociation. For your little one's pain, or for shame, a good reparenting therapist may fit better. The same goes for attachment wounds or abandoning yourself, or not having an inner adult you can count on.\n\n### Related Clinical Work\n\nThis framework developed through the teachers and collaborators credited below and my own practice. Later revisions also compare notes with other approaches, like schema therapy, compassion-focused therapy, attachment repair, motivational interviewing, mentalizing, metacognitive therapy, behavioral activation, radically open DBT, emotion-focused therapy, spiritually integrated psychotherapy and so on. I borrow particular distinctions and practices rather than presenting the whole synthesis as their consensus or claiming that their research validates it as a package.\n\n## Teachers and Practice Resources\n\nDaja works with inner-child pl/ork intuitively, through the divine Nurturer and Protector archetypes. Her work is less neuroscience and CBT and more direct archetypal practice, and the three grown-up jobs draw a lot from it. She has a [book](https://a.co/d/7Sa4kP2) and a [free one-hour YouTube training](https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PL_kbcFWhZK0wJeFUShGvHFZa1u3x7p8Y-&si=Fr0g76mDh8-WLMjM), and she's on [Instagram](http://instagram.com/ihopeyoufindserenity).\n\nLisa Romano brings more neuroscience and CBT, and her [video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2tbMRZKdHJ4) is a good place to start. She makes the point that remembering the specific details of childhood trauma isn't necessary, because the felt body knows what needs to come up. [Key](https://open.substack.com/users/432335983-key-to-enlightenment?utm_source=mentions) developed the Guide's sense of direction, and the identity and differentiation work from Bowen I mentioned earlier. She led a [guided inner-child meditation](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UkVlDixqqA0) in Nairobi in January 2026 for twenty people, and it's up on YouTube.\n\nI don't know of a resource to find a perfect inner child therapist, and there are none which work with my guide yet, (although I can consult, and if you like the guide, you can ask a therapist to check it out and see what they agree with or disagree with as a great way to vet them). The best I could suggest at the moment is to search [Open Path Collective](https://openpathcollective.org/pricing-and-eligibility-for-affordable-therapy/) (or a similar resource in your country), which lists thousands of affordable therapists. Filter for  Schema Therapy, since that's the one with the most similarity to my guide, but you'll find about 10x more Internal Family Systems therapists, which also overlap with reparenting in many ways. You can often find [even more Schema therapists at the official site](https://schematherapysociety.org/Search?&tab=1): 3x more on that page for NYC than via Open Path search when I checked. And for bottom-up support, there's my [Somatic Therapies Guide](http://somatic.u-dont-exist.com/), the same map as in Your Body Might Need Some Love First.", "fed821edb8cfbd24794ecb3e55bb30c0751b1d5fd304e26c48d1851ddb3a5f22"], ["t72a_h1sp", () => "# Simple Practices\n\n## Borrow Hindsight\n\nI sometimes ask: \"How would I respond to this once I understood it more clearly, in balance and with compassion?\" It's a question borrowed from your future self. It can change how the whole thing looks. It can also just make you feel a bit more like that person, or less alone (sometimes all at once). Keep in mind it's only their best guess. No perfect Higher Self is handing down answers.\n\nThat future you also knows why you can't pull this off perfectly today. What would they do for you, if they were here? Maybe they'd be kind about it, or maybe they'd just make you some food. If you can't picture them at all, make the food yourself (a \"no\" or a pause counts as food here), and skip pretending to feel some way you don't.\n\nYou can borrow a wiser future version of whoever else is involved, too, as long as you don't assume they'll end up on your side, or sorry. What the current one keeps doing is still what you go by, however good the picture looks (the future one has never called you back).\n\n## Remember When You Already Did It\n\nMy father often suggested folks remember a similar-level (or even harder) challenge that they were able to handle. Maybe you survived a car crash and surgery a few years ago, but now you're having trouble with emotional eating. You can draw strength from the past. As my sister, a historian, likes to say, [\"The past didn't go anywhere.\"](https://theanarchistlibrary.org/library/april-rosenblum-the-past-didn-t-go-anywhere) Your own challenge doesn't need to be as dramatic as that, as long as it really happened. So who or what helped you get through it? Is one piece of that still within reach? If you catch yourself thinking \"I survived a car crash, so why can't I stop eating when I'm upset?\", that's the past turned into a stick to hit yourself with. Maybe you're more worn out now, or missing whatever helped back then. (No example? Try another doorway.)\n\n## A Heart-to-Child Loop\n\nWith a little warmth to work with, rest one hand at the center of your chest (the heart chakra) and feel for love, and the other on the solar plexus, just below, and feel for your little one. The solar plexus is the most vulnerable part of the torso. One punch there hurts a lot, as I sadly learned while getting beaten up every day at kindergarten recess by a gang organized by my best friend. Can you believe it? So it makes sense to look for your little one there.\n\nPicture the love traveling from your heart down to your little one. If some comes back up, let it, and send it on down again, so it goes around like a loop. That part's a gift from them, and they don't owe it to you, so if nothing comes back, just keep sending yours. No warmth today? Then rest your hands there and notice what you find, no faking required. Non-judgement is a place to start. It can blossom into love over time.\n\n## Journal With Each Hand\n\nLucia Capacchione, an art therapist, wrote a whole book on this one, [The Power of Your Other Hand](https://www.luciac.com/). As titles go, it's pretty literal. Your other hand, the clumsy one, writes like a first grader anyway, so it gets the scared or hurt feelings (nothing made up). The usual hand answers, as the grown-up. Some people who get nothing but silence from their little one are surprised by what the other hand comes up with. A protective part might grab the pen first, and then it gets that hand until it's had its say.\n\nAnd what if all that writing leaves you more confused than when you started? Then just keep track for a while, and save the patterns for later, if they turn out to matter at all. A memory aid doesn't have to explain your life. It could be a dated note of what happened, or of what helped. A reminder or a next step works too. Or you can leave yourself a voice note.\n\n## Just Like Me\n\nThis one works best on a steadier day, when thinking about other people won't pull you out of your own shoes. Name the feeling plainly. Lonely, say. Then think of how many people are feeling that exact thing right now, and wanting it to stop, just like you. Some of them make it worse trying to get away from it. Just like you, sometimes. \ud83d\ude05 Wish all of you, you first, enough support to stay with it, so the pain stops with you.\n\nIf you've tried Pema Ch\u00f6dr\u00f6n's [tonglen](https://www.lionsroar.com/how-to-practice-tonglen-meditation/), this is a gentler cousin, and normal breathing is fine. Whoever hurt you might come to mind, too. Forgiving them isn't part of it, and neither is picturing their childhood. Getting in touch is a separate decision, for a much calmer day, if it ever comes. With that many people feeling the same thing, the theory that you're the defective one gets kind of hard to keep up.\n\n## \u201cAnd So That\u2019s the Way It Happened!\u201d\n\nMy ex, Xandria, taught me to append \u201cAnd so that\u2019s the way it happened!\u201d to a difficult memory. Say it the way a grandpa would finish a story on the porch. (\"Once upon a time\u2026\" helps if the start is where you get stuck.) Somehow it moves the whole thing out of the courtroom, where the case was still being argued, and into your life story. Pick a memory you can handle for the first few tries.\n\nDoes saying it make the memory fiction? Well, no, it's still what happened, as far as you remember it, even if some details turn out fuzzy. And nobody's off the hook (you included, if you were the one who did the harm): whatever repair is owed is still owed. If the phrase does the trick, leave it at that, no need to narrate the whole thing.\n\nDo keep an ear on who's narrating, though. If the voice starts sneering, like it's making fun of the younger you, stop right there (that's the courtroom creeping back in). Same if you feel more ashamed, or kind of floaty and unreal, since more distance won't help with that. And when it goes well, it's a good time to check in with the younger you in that story. What do they need from you now?\n\n## One Intention in the Morning, One Review at Night\n\nIn the morning, begin the day intentionally, not just because the sun chose to come up once more, since we can't count on that \ud83e\udee3. Here's one way you might pl/orkfully start off:\n\n\u201cToday I will neither be Peter Pan nor Captain Hook. I will notice a hook or 2 which lead to unconscious behaviors.\u201d\n\nThen at night, pay yourself to be your own therapist so as not to break the bank. Equipoisedly look into how the practice went for you today:\n\n\"Where did I notice the hook?\" At dinner with the in-laws? Doom scrolling TikTok nonsense? Watching anim\u00e9?\n\n\"Where did it still seem to control me despite noticing it?\" (That's the most frustrating part, when you know better, and you still act the same.)\n\nThen get practical so it doesn't become another OCD loop: \"What small repair or protective act shall I bring in for a proper response to that?\"\n\nAnd don't forget to appreciate the good! Moments of warm, beneficial connection (whether internally from child to adult, socially, spiritually, or undefined) are all to be celebrated. Were you maybe firm like an elephant today, or courageous like a lion? Adopt the entire spirit animal zoo if need be! Turtles are good if you need impulse control.\n\nCount recognition as progress, regardless whether it came up a bit late, as the goal is to bring the awareness closer and closer to the present. All or nothing from the get go would be cool, but we normally have to learn step by step. If/when you interrupted the pattern, pat yourself on the back, or even kiss yourself on the shoulder. If you made a mistake, you can see how to repair that. You can renew your intention for tomorrow, getting better & better, every day, in every way. And enjoy your well-earned rest tonight.\n\n## Use Childhood Photographs Gently\n\nMaybe you've tried picturing your little one and thought, \"This feels fake. I'm just making it up.\" A real photo of you as a child can help with that, since there's no face or posture to invent. Yes, yes, it might feel a bit strange, seeing your own face from back then. But look at it gently and just notice what comes up.\n\nWhile you look, notice what your body does and what feelings or urges show up. Is there something that younger you seems to want you to understand? Some of it you'll remember yourself, and some of it you might even glean from talking to family/friends from then (\"You hated that coat\"). It might just be an inference you're guessing from the picture, or perhaps it triggers an image or a dream. Or just nothing comes up, which is also completely OK. Ask what your little one needs from you now, and do a bit of it.\n\nWe don't need to ask it leading questions, like \"Who abused me & how?\" or \"What memory is my body hiding?\" Grilling one photo until a story shows up is a good way to get a story, but stories aren't necessarily the truth, and even if they are accurate, the feelings beneath them are still more important. Memory is an amorphous thing, easily influenced by guided imagery and suggestions, so we gotta be careful about that. [Look at this study, for example](https://link.springer.com/article/10.3758/BF03196318), where people were shown a doctored photo of a childhood hot-air balloon ride they never took. Half of them actually started sorta remembering it. I'm sure you can relate.\n\nA photo is just one moment that somebody picked and framed... not necessarily representative. Flipping thru an album may give different jolts in your body.\n\nIf you start to suspect someone of something serious, like abusing you, please don't confront or accuse anyone based only on what came up here. You might be looking at the photo thinking, \"That would explain so much.\" Journal what came up and how it came up, and see if you can get support from someone level-headed who won't lead you toward an answer.\n\nPut the photo back in its drawer when you're finished. If it starts tugging at you, so you keep going back to search it or you need to know for sure, or you're getting overwhelmed, take a break from it right then. Settle yourself in the present to close out properly. Re-evaluation counseling always ends on this \"Present Time\" reminder. Can be as simple and silly as \"Oh, Today is Monday, my favorite day of the week!\"\n\n## Close the Session and Return to Life\n\nThat \"Present Time\" ending is worth stealing for every session, photos or no photos. Remember the checking from When Healing Turns Into Checking? Without an ending, the processing can slide right back into that. So when you're done, look around and say where you are. Feel your feet on the floor, or the chair under you, and unclench your jaw and hands, since they've probably been at it this whole time.\n\nAnd what's next for you two? Maybe it's making food, or just going to bed. Whatever it is, tell your little one, \"That's enough for today. We're going back to the life we're building.\" Nothing has to be resolved first, and whatever you're still feeling can come along. Going back to your life is part of the healing, too.", "20466cec28d02ac1ddec1e461bcc9ed86fc4097782ce907cdd8f086870905616"], ["t72a_h1mj", () => "# My Journey\n\nI used to preach [Buddhist enlightenment](http://mindset.u-dont-exist.com/) a lot. But an old childhood wound could still hit and suddenly I was the same little kid again, even when the spiritual part of me felt completely free.\n\nThe way I picture it is almost stupidly simple. Imagine you're broke and starving, and your baby is starving too. *Understandable*: there is no food. Then a food bank opens nearby and suddenly you've got lots of nutritious munchies. But you're eating all of it yourself while baby you is still starving. *Unforgivable.*\n\nNobody leaves a baby starving on purpose. I just didn't believe there was a baby. Inner-child stuff sounded silly to me until I could actually feel him. Then there was this little me actually hurting, and I could send some of the love I'd already found his way.\n\nI think most people who chase spiritual freedom are also trying to get away from something painful in themselves, and a lot of it goes back to childhood. That's [spiritual bypassing](https://goenka.u-dont-exist.com/). Plenty of seekers and gurus do it too; I've written about the [Dalai Lama as one example](https://substack.com/@udontexist/note/c-245314772?r=5vdc6m). I did it too. It doesn't mean you're stupid or crazy.\n\nSpending your whole life processing your wounded inner child isn't freedom either. Spirituality opened a much bigger horizon for me\u2014love and happiness beyond the conditions of my life story. Maybe ordinary life already gives you enough love and meaning. Great. It didn't for me. Conditional love felt radically insufficient, so I kept looking for something deeper.\n\nI was well-loved as a child, but from age twelve to twenty-six I was depressed and cried every night from feeling the pain of the world. [Loving-kindness (metta) meditation](http://love.u-dont-exist.com/) healed that depression almost instantly for me.\n\nI wasn't lacking love. My parents had given me a lot of it. I found more in [Buddhist teachings\u2014which, it turns out, also include inner-child self-love!](https://substack.com/profile/355021294-u-dont-existcom/note/c-297324975) and later in [loveyhuasca](http://soma.u-dont-exist.com/). Metta gave all that love somewhere to land.\n\nBut it didn't reach everything. A lot of the child stuff around my attachment to women was still there. When I later tried [self-directed inner-child therapy](https://youtu.be/yEWFjODXJbI?si=IXUqrhExojOm6Itm), it felt like I was making it up at first. I used San Pedro cactus and CBD tea with sublingual mapacho, and that cracked it open; structured sessions with an inner-child therapist deepened it.\n\nI found the therapist, Daja, years later, randomly, and gave it a shot, although I didn't think I needed it. But she did actually help me see some things more clearly. For example, she had me write down how many people I could fully trust as a child (lots), then how many people I could fully trust as an adult (zero). That was difficult, and it's actually the opposite of what you might expect from inner-child therapy, since usually that's about people who had problems trusting as a child.\n\nWhat it showed me is that I was ignoring a lot of things that were really important to look at, and that was one of them. I wouldn't have even thought to ask the question she asked me if I were doing it on my own. It's also an example of how therapy can sometimes make you feel worse than before, when you become more aware of problems. But personally I prefer to see things as they are, so I can figure out how to work with them, rather than just hide in delusions.\n\nIt also showed me that I really do need to count more on myself than I assumed. I need to be much better as an inner adult for my inner child, because my childhood was actually so nice that I kind of just assume I can trust everyone, and that gets me into trouble. So it got me curious to learn more about inner-child therapy in general and map it out completely, which is a process I'm still in the midst of as I test this guide with people.\n\nThe image below maps out the details as best as I can fit:\n\n### Don\u2019t Give the Inner Adult Away\n\nIf you still feel like a kid inside, somebody who seems grown-up can feel like salvation, and letting them do some of the grown-up jobs for you can actually help for a while. But they can know things you don't and still be wrong about you. The test is whether the relationship makes me more grounded, truthful, and free.\n\nHow do you check that? I'd keep whatever you feel around them, and look at their track record. Do they respect a no, or keep pushing on it, and what happens when you disagree? If something outside of them backs up what they're telling you, and it all adds up, that's a good sign.\n\nEven [Pema Ch\u00f6dr\u00f6n](https://pemachodronfoundation.org/product/taking-the-leap-book/), who inspired parts of this guide, comes from a tradition where devotion to a teacher is a big deal, and she still says a teacher should \u201cwean us from dependency altogether\u201d and help us grow up.\n\nSay you grew up learning that what you wanted didn't matter. Now you're only just finding out what you actually think, maybe even learning to say no. Then somebody tells you there is no self. That might be freeing at another point in your life; right now it can sound a lot like the message you already know: what you want doesn't matter. Or you tell a therapist, \u201cNo, that's not right for me,\u201d and they call it resistance. Maybe they're right. But maybe they're wrong about you, or taking you somewhere you don't want to go. You still have to be able to say so.\n\nBorrow adulthood. Do not surrender it.", "af8b4bce3bc8e0efec73d44884791c46a9e4d64c2ef433268b653ed8402f1393"], ["t72a_h1ce", () => "# The Chicken-and-Egg Problem\n\nC\u00e9line's song is called *On ne change pas*\u2014\"we don't change.\" I think that's both fortunately and unfortunately true for most of us. Fortunately in the sense that the sweet, innocent, curious, vivacious little one is still inside us, even if hiding. Unfortunate in the sense that we often don't truly develop the adult qualities that we might pretend to have, so we continue burdening that little one inside us with troubles they aren't made for handling. This shows up in how we may feel abandoned, helpless, impulsive, frightened, or desperate to be chosen.\n\nAnd I realized that it doesn't make sense to ask the child to heal itself, which is really a disguised request for the child to abandon its own nature and turn into an adult. There was never anything wrong with the child's worth to begin with. Instead, we need to separately develop the qualities of a good inner parent for that rightly vulnerable child: the loving Nurturer, safe Protector, and wise Guide.\n\nThat's one grown-up doing three jobs, not three more people moving in, and each job keeps the others in balance. Say a party gets to be too much for your little one. The Protector gets you both out of there, and on the way home the Nurturer tells them they did great, even though mostly they hid in the bathroom. And the Guide helps them think twice about the next weird party they might go to, avoiding another cycle of bathroom vacationing. \ud83d\ude48\n\nThe Guide is the one people overlook the most. I left it out of my first version of this guide until Key pointed it out, and without an inner Guide, it's easy to go looking for one in a cult. Our lives can become small and like a never-ending therapy session when we focus only on caring for ourselves rather than giving ourselves a mission as well. That's the Guide's job. But then some people over-rely on their mission to avoid caring for themselves.\n\nIf the word \"parent\" calls up the wrong archetype for you, pick another one. Your future self might work, or the captain of a ship, so long as they'd do those three jobs.\n\nBut here\u2019s the chicken-and-egg problem. What if you\u2019re so far inside the child-state that you can\u2019t become the adult? This is much easier when you can already reach one of those adult roles. But when you can't, it's like you sit there trying to be loving while your mind is becoming controlled again by the inherited critic voice, or you start going numb, throwing a fit, or panicking.\n\nSometimes somebody else may have to do one part of the adult role with you or for you first. You get to feel what that is like and watch how they do it. Maybe you copy one small thing you've seen them do, or imagine them there when they're not, and try one piece yourself while they're still helping with the rest. That's what I mean by borrowed adulthood. You may still need help with almost all of it for a while, but over time there are more and more of those little parts you can do yourself.\n\nFor now, I think of the whole path in five fairly predictable stages. You might be starting at any point here (and it may change even from hour to hour as well):\n\n1. You're still fully identified as the child \u2014 use your childlike superpowers to come into the \"Wow of Now\" (have you seen the movie Waking Life?), and make sure there's enough safety around you to continue:\n2. Begin to borrow adulthood \u2014 remember watching Mr. Rogers or Sesame Street? Those adult role models are what we're looking for at this stage. This can almost feel like playing house as a kid where you imagined how you'd take care of a baby doll etc. \n3. Practice adulting \u2014 do at least one part of caring for yourself while support is still there from the previous step.\n4. Adult mode itself \u2014 after sufficient practice with reparenting, you become a natural at sending love to little you, protecting them, and directing them. \n5. Adult and child in relationship \u2014 the adult leads. The child isn't pushed away, but doesn't have to run the whole life alone. This can actually feel really weird if you've been stuck in adult mode for a while. Our goal is to go from weird to silly and playful. Lose self-consciousness while gaining self-awareness, if that makes sense!\n\nWhichever stage you're at, saying the right thing when you sit down with your little one helps, but you also have to start actually doing some of it in the rest of your life. Your little one starts believing the grown-up you when they catch you lying down because you're wiped out, instead of pushing through one more chore.", "a9025a8cb5a5a025eb9b2d975548fc9e436dd63124ec0461c45d225815d4b458"], ["t72a_h1st", () => "# Speak Toward What You Are Building\n\nIt's not so hard to understand that words have power. They point your attention somewhere, so aim them at what you want more of, whether that's something you'd like to be able to do or a relationship. Say you tell yourself \"I'm not dependent.\" Well, now you're thinking about being dependent. Being totally independent also isn't realistic nor ideal, and can even seem like just a reaction to the original question. We can often strike a nice nuanced poetic balance, like: \"I'm provided for by the universe, like the birds & lilies Jesus reminded us of.\" With your little one, and in hypnosis especially, make it easy to picture: warmth, protection, steadiness, rest, whatever's really needed to melt into your happy place. You'll save a ton of money on vacations!\n\nBut let's also make sure we don't hide from shadow stuff with toxic positivity. Grief is grief, and letting tears flow can be immensely therapeutic, as long as they don't hijack your perspective with a cemented story of what will always be true. If your little one says \"he hurt me,\" that's harm, and it gets called harm, even if it was also a learning experience. Same for anger, a no, who's responsible, what's possible in practice, all that. Then once the difficult experience is acknowledged by the Nurturer, the Protector & Guide parts can lead the way up to more safety and maturity.\n\nBefore giving anyone advice, your little one included, it can help to find out what kind of company they want: \"Do you want me to mostly listen, help you untangle this, give you ideas, or some mix?\" Not that you have to make a ritual of asking every time. If it's obvious what they want, go with that. If it isn't, that one sentence can keep you from advising somebody who's thinking, \"I just need you to hear this.\" Or from just listening while they're asking for help thinking.\n\nWhat they tell you is worth remembering, too, until they say something different. (And don't guess it from their gender or some other stereotype.) Same with advice you've already given: if nothing new has changed it, a quick \"like I said\" and moving on works better than offering it again like it's new.\n\nThe basic premise of [Hearthwork](https://hearthwork.u-dont-exist.com), my peer-counseling framework, is: Equal time. Attention without interpretation, advice, or fixing. You speak. I listen. Then we switch. Presence, Warmth, Discipline, Confidentiality. It's kind of a third type of relationship, alongside ordinary friendship and professional therapy, and one your little one might really need, because modern life rarely gives you anything like it.\n\nSome of what the three grown-up jobs do can come from outside you, too. When you take turns listening with a good peer, as equals, their warmth and the way they stay with you lend you the Nurturer for a while. Keeping what you say private, and stopping when the time's up, lends you the Protector. If you say no, they don't push. But they don't act as your Guide, or decide what your experience means. Maybe they're thinking, \"Oh, I know what this is about,\" or \"Here's what you should do.\" They keep it to themselves. A question from them at the right moment can help you hear your own Guide, and what you answer is up to you. Then you trade places, and it's your turn to keep \"Here's what you should do\" to yourself.\n\nThe peer doesn't become your permanent parent, though. They show you a capacity, help you get the hang of it, and give it back to you, which is borrowed adulthood in its healthiest form (a driving instructor isn't planning to drive you around forever either). Is that therapy? Well, not the mainstream kind, but yes, for sure. Professional therapy's still useful for what it does, and peers and community do something else, so a serious healing path can include both.\n\nCommunity can come before the big opening or after it, too. Sometimes loving people give somebody enough safety and belonging that a deeper opening becomes possible. Other times it's the other way around. Somebody has a big opening first, a love or a meaning they couldn't have imagined before, and then they start to \"manifest\" different kinds of people and relationships in their life. Either way, the community is what helps make it practical, real, down to earth, embodied and so on.\n\nThis is part of why I connect inner-child pl/ork with Hearthwork and with building community. Having a beautiful experience on the inside isn't really the point. The love has to move, and it doesn't always go the same way round (the door you came in through matters less than the loop). From a spiritual experience to your little one, then into your relationship with the grown-up you, then out to other people, and back again.", "42f92e8a8cbb38e01e2247f5709e7a1d09d5daa370eae8e97cd85e039350b99c"]];
const rev = !!window.__reverse;
const items = rev ? items0.slice().reverse() : items0;
const order0 = items0.map(x => x[0]);
const lane = k => "t72a" + '_' + k;
try { localStorage.removeItem(lane(rev ? 'rev' : 'fwd')); } catch (e) {}
window.__t72a = [];
const log = m => { window.__t72a.push(m); try { localStorage.setItem(lane(rev ? 'rev' : 'fwd'), JSON.stringify(window.__t72a)); } catch (e) {} };
const other = () => { try { return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(lane(rev ? 'fwd' : 'rev')) || '[]').filter(m => / (clicked|taking)$/.test(m)).map(m => order0.indexOf(m.split(':')[0])); } catch (e) { return []; } };
const setter = Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(HTMLTextAreaElement.prototype, 'value').set;
const sha = async t => Array.from(new Uint8Array(await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', new TextEncoder().encode(t)))).map(b => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');
const button = () => [...document.querySelectorAll('button[type=submit]')].find(b => /check for ai/i.test(b.innerText));
const wait = ms => new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms));
async function run() {
  for (const [k, mk, want] of items) {
    const o = other(), i = order0.indexOf(k);
    if (o.length && (rev ? i <= Math.max(...o) : i >= Math.min(...o))) { log('stop at ' + k + ': the other tab has the rest'); break; }
    log(k + ': taking');  // claimed before the work, so the other tab never takes it too (turn 65: with a margin
                          // of one instead, both tabs stopped short of the last check)
    const T = mk();  // built when its turn comes, so it can use texts stored earlier in this batch
    for (let j = 0; j < 20 && !(button() && document.querySelector('textarea')); j++) await wait(500);
    const ta = document.querySelector('textarea');
    if (!ta) { log(k + ': no textarea'); break; }
    setter.call(ta, T); ta.dispatchEvent(new Event('input', {bubbles: true}));
    let b = null;
    for (let j = 0; j < 30; j++) { await wait(500); b = button(); if (b && !b.disabled) break; }
    const h = await sha(ta.value);
    if (h !== want) { log(k + ': hash mismatch ' + h + ' (is its prefix in sessionStorage?)'); break; }
    if (!b || b.disabled) { log(k + ': button not ready'); break; }
    try { sessionStorage.setItem(k, T); } catch (e) {}
    b.click(); log(k + ': clicked');
    await wait(10000);
  }
  log('end');
}
run(); 'started'
